(*
porta space-sync — an exchange is SIX spans and `pushBuild` is three more nested
inside it, and each one fires exactly once per exchange.

Why this exists: the first span capture off c6-bench
(docs/results/2026-08-05-c6-span-capture-first.md) found a check-in costing ~300 ms
and being 97% of the tick, with NO breakdown inside it — pull versus push, radio
versus VM, send versus wait, all invisible, because `checkIn` was one span measured
from the app. The breakdown needs spans INSIDE PortaBridge, which is what this
test pins.

The six PARTITION the exchange, which since rete#440 R2 is spread over three
reactions — the check-in, the #batch's doorbell, the #ack's doorbell — so the
waits run from a send to the reaction that receives the reply:

  pullSend   encode the #pull frame and send it
  pullWait   from the send to the reaction that receives the #batch
  applyIn    apply the batch, publish sys/metrics
  pushBuild  collectOutbound + encode + the trim re-encodes
  pushSend   send the #push frame
  pushWait   from the send to the reaction that applies the #ack

And tuvm#161 splits `pushBuild` the same way, because it measured 62% of the
check-in on c6-bench and the source says a value is CBOR-encoded three or more
times per push:

  pushCollect  collectOutbound — the scans and `valueFits:`, which encodes every
               candidate value in full and discards the bytes
  pushEncode   the first full-frame encode, encoding those values again
  pushTrim     the re-encode-per-dropped-row loop

Why the SENDS are separated from the WAITS, which is the whole point: the pull
frame is ~40 B and rides one 6LoWPAN fragment, while a push frame runs to the
512 B cap and rides five or six — and a datagram survives only if every fragment
does. So `pushSend` is the one stretch where fragmentation and MAC retries can
hide. A coarser "radio wait versus everything else" split puts both sends on the
VM side of the line and would report a fragmentation cost as interpreter cost.

`spans` is a CONSTRUCTOR ARGUMENT and not optional, for the reason src/span.h
gives for pinning the two C-owned slots: a node must not be able to ship an
uninstrumented bridge by forgetting a bracket.

What is asserted, and why each is falsifiable:

- `slots 11` — 2 reserved (C-owned) + the bridge's 9, registered AT CONSTRUCTION.
  Registering lazily on first use would grow the table mid-window, and
  SpanCapture publishes one cell per slot — so a capture would publish a
  different set of cells at stop than the set that existed at start.
- `virgin ... 0` — registration alone records no sample. Falsifies a bracket that
  fires at register: time.
- `after two ... 2` — one sample per span per exchange, across TWO exchanges. A
  bracket left open across the boundary records nothing on the next `begin:`
  (span_end no-ops on a closed slot), so it would read 1, not 2.
- `pushTrim` reading 2 IS AN ASSERTION, not a side effect. This frame is far
  under the 512 B cap, so the trim loop never drops a row — and the span still
  has to fire, because a conditional bracket would make "the loop never ran" and
  "the span is missing" the same reading in the capture.

The fake socket is doorbell-driven: the test hands it the next reply and rings
its channel, and the bridge reads it to empty — #batch, then #ack, twice, the
real reply sequence, so no leg ever reaches its budget.

expect:
slots 11
virgin 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0
after two 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2
*)
class BridgeSpanBrackets packageUsing: manifest = () (

  (* Duck-typed stand-ins, as in 06_reply_tag_match: PortaBridge only ever sends
     `sendTo:port:data:`, `receive` and `doorbell` to its socket, and `data` to
     what receive answers. *)
  public class FakeDatagram = ( | private data | ) (
    public setData: d = ( data:: d. ^ self )
    public data = ( ^ data )
  ) : (
    public on: d = ( ^ self new setData: d )
  )

  (* One queued datagram: `hold:` queues it, `receive` answers it once and then
     nil, which is what an emptied socket looks like. *)
  public class FakeSocket = ( | private queued | ) (
    public doorbell = ( ^ 'test/net' )
    public hold: bytes = ( queued:: bytes. ^ self )
    public sendTo: host port: p data: bytes = ( ^ self )
    public receive = ( | q |
      queued == nil ifTrue: [ ^ nil ].
      q:: queued. queued:: nil.
      ^ FakeDatagram on: q )
  ) : ()

  ring: sock with: bytes space: space = (
    sock hold: bytes.
    space out: 'test/net' value: 1.
    space drain.
    ^ self )

  (* register: is idempotent by name, so this answers the slot the BRIDGE was
     given rather than a new one — which is also the only way a test can ask for
     it without the bridge exposing six accessors nothing else would use. *)
  nOf: spans named: aName = (
    ^ (spans statsAt: (spans register: aName)) basicAt: 1 )

  line: label spans: spans = (
    ^ label ,
      ' ' , (self nOf: spans named: #pullSend)  printString ,
      ' ' , (self nOf: spans named: #pullWait)  printString ,
      ' ' , (self nOf: spans named: #applyIn)   printString ,
      ' ' , (self nOf: spans named: #pushBuild) printString ,
      ' ' , (self nOf: spans named: #pushSend)  printString ,
      ' ' , (self nOf: spans named: #pushWait)  printString ,
      ' ' , (self nOf: spans named: #pushCollect) printString ,
      ' ' , (self nOf: spans named: #pushEncode)  printString ,
      ' ' , (self nOf: spans named: #pushTrim)    printString )

  public main: platform args: args = (
    | space spans bridge batch ack sock |
    space:: platform space.
    spans:: platform Spans new.

    (* Something for the push leg to send, so `pushFrame` is not nil and the
       second half of each check-in actually runs. sys/metrics is written by the
       bridge on every check-in anyway, but this makes the first one independent
       of that. *)
    space put: 'obs/health' value: 11.

    batch:: platform Cbor encode:
      (platform Record tag: #batch
         with: #hi -> 7
         with: #ent -> (platform List with:
            (platform List with: 7 with: 'goal/name' with: 2 with: 'bench'))).
    ack:: platform Cbor encode: (platform Record tag: #ack with: #hw -> 1).

    sock:: FakeSocket new.
    bridge:: platform PortaBridge
      socket: sock space: space id: 'n1' peer: '::1' port: 6969 spans: spans timers: platform timers.

    ('slots ' , spans slotCount printString) println.
    (self line: 'virgin' spans: spans) println.

    bridge checkIn.
    self ring: sock with: batch space: space.
    self ring: sock with: ack space: space.
    bridge checkIn.
    self ring: sock with: batch space: space.
    self ring: sock with: ack space: space.
    (self line: 'after two' spans: spans) println.
    ^ 0
  )
) : (
  public packageUsing: manifest = ( ^ self new )
)
